// KNOWN GAP — this program documents a real hole in the checker, filed rather than
// fixed this round. It is in should_pass because that is what the checker currently
// (incorrectly) does with it, NOT because this file's shape is actually sound.
//
// `a / b` is a Result<Int, DivByZero>, not an Int (§5.3). The checker catches this when
// a division result is used directly as a binop operand without `?` (see
// tests/should_fail/expr_div_no_try.fit), but it has no general call-argument or
// return-type compatibility check — only resource typestate is checked at call sites,
// and tail-expression/return types are never compared to the declared return type at
// all (see the "KNOWN GAP" comments in src/checker.ts, near checkFn and near the
// call-argument loop in checkExpr's "call" case).
//
// So `take(a / b)` below checks with zero errors, even though `take` expects an Int and
// receives a Result<Int, DivByZero>. `fit codegen` on this file produces C that fails to
// compile under `-Werror` (passing a struct where an int is expected). Building general
// argument/return type-checking is out of scope for this round — it's a separate, much
// larger concern the checker has never attempted anywhere else, and a narrow fix just
// for Result-vs-plain would be an inconsistent partial implementation of that larger
// concern. This file exists so a future round doesn't have to rediscover the gap from
// scratch.
fn take(n: Int) -> ()

fn bad_call(a: Int, b: Int) -> () {
    take(a / b)
}
